<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Long-term action recognition (LTAR) is challenging due to extended temporal spans with complex atomic action correlations and visual confounders. Although vision-language models (VLMs) have shown promise, they often rely on statistical correlations instead of causal mechanisms. Moreover, existing causality-based methods address modal-specific biases but lack cross-modal causal modeling, limiting their utility in VLM-based LTAR. This paper proposes <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ross-<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odal <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ual-<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning (CMDCL), which introduces a structural causal model to uncover causal relationships between videos and label texts.
CMDCL addresses cross-modal biases in text embeddings via textual causal intervention and removes confounders inherent in the visual modality through visual causal intervention guided by the debiased text.
These dual-causal interventions enable robust action representations to address LTAR challenges. Experimental results on three benchmarks including Charades, Breakfast and COIN, demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed model. Our code is available at https://github.com/xushaowu/CMDCL.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>causal learning, cross-modal learning, vision-language model, action recognition, long-term action
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spatiotemporal representation learning has significantly advanced human action recognition in videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shamil et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Long-term action recognition (LTAR) remains more challenging than its short-term counterpart <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryoo et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryoo et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, requiring modeling complex correlations among atomic actions over extended temporal spans while facing increased vulnerability to visual confounders (e.g., background and attire) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Vision-language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> have recently emerged as powerful tools, extracting robust visual features through alignment with textual category descriptors. Fine-tuning VLM visual encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mondal et al., 2023</a>)</cite> has proven effective for action recognition.
However, existing research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>)</cite> indicates that VLMs often rely on spurious statistical correlations (e.g., superficial patterns like frequently co-occurring objects/backgrounds) instead of invariant causal mechanisms (e.g., essential relationships like atomic action sequences or physical constraints). This fundamentally limits their ability to discover invariant latent factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While VLMs demonstrate strong spatial vision-text alignment, their limitations become apparent in LTAR’s inherent core requirements: modeling temporal atomic action correlations and suppressing visual confounders. As Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a) illustrates, LTAR involves both sequential causality (e.g., “butter pan” preceding “crack egg” in “make scrambled egg”, versus the reverse order in “make pancake”) and co-occurring causality among atomic actions. This causal diversity amplifies cross-modal bias in VLM-based models, where visually prominent but causally irrelevant atomic actions become spuriously associated with textual embeddings of semantically unrelated long-term actions.
Consequently, as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c), VLM-based models exhibit an overreliance on visually distinctive yet noncausal actions such as prioritizing “add salt &amp; pepper” for “make scrambled egg”, which leads to suboptimal LTAR performance.
Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) further reveals co-occurring causality’s vulnerability to visual confounders: exclusive atomic actions like “stirfry egg” are often confused with visually similar counterparts like “fry pancake” due to shared contextual features.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06603v1/fig1_visualization_suprious_instance.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/141;" width="476" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Challenges in capturing true causality with VLM-based methods.
<span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Causal factors and confounders in long-term actions:</span> Using “make scrambled egg” as an example, “butter pan” preceding “crack egg” illustrates sequential causality, while “put egg to plate” and “stirfry egg” demonstrate co-occurring causality. In contrast, “add salt &amp; pepper” lacks logical causality and acts as a confounder, akin to background noise. Atomic actions are further classified as exclusive or nonexclusive based on whether they appear only within specific long-term actions. <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) Co-occurrence frequency statistics:</span> Analysis of the Breakfast dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuehne et al., 2014</a>)</cite> shows high co-occurrence frequencies for exclusive atomic actions within respective long-term actions (i.e., “make scrambled egg” and “make pancake”), but visually similar exclusive actions across different long-term actions (e.g., “stirfry egg” and “fry pancake”) pose challenges to VLMs that rely on cross-modal statistical correlations. <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c) Cross-modal matching probability statistics:</span> Matching probabilities between visual embeddings of atomic actions and textual embeddings of long-term actions using a pretrained VLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> reveal that, except for “add salt &amp; pepper,” most atomic actions in “make scrambled egg” correlate more with “make pancake” than with “make scrambled egg.” This highlights VLMs’ failure to capture sequential and co-occurring causalities, overemphasizing visually distinctive but noncausal actions. </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06603v1/fig2_causal_graph_vs_normal.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/171;" width="381" height="171"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The illustration of structural causal models (SCMs). (a) SCM of conventional video action recognition models. (b) SCM of pretrained VLM-based LTAR models. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under the potential interplay between cross-modal bias and visual confounders, achieving optimal LTAR performance in VLM-based models requires Structural Causal Model (SCM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf, 2022</a>)</cite> to mitigate spurious statistical correlations.
However, conventional SCM, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a), fails to characterize VLM-based LTAR. The causal factor <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in LTAR must encode both spatial semantics and temporal action correlations (e.g., sequential or co-occurring). When VLMs align <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with text features <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, they may overemphasize spatial matching while ignoring temporal dependencies, introducing cross-modal bias <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> that stems from spurious visual-textual correlations (e.g., frequent co-occurrence of kitchen background and cooking actions).
This requires extending SCM to split the confounder <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> into cross-modal bias <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and inherent visual confounders <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b), <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> create backdoor paths <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V,T\}\leftarrow B\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V,T\}\leftarrow B\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\leftarrow Z\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\leftarrow Z\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which lead the model to learn spurious correlations instead of true causal relationships.
While recent methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> pay attention to recovering causal features in video tasks through causal interventions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2016</a>)</cite>, they remain limited for LTAR due to the lack of explicit cross-modal causal modeling and difficulty disentangling spurious correlations in untrimmed long-term videos.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ross-<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odal <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ual-<span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning (CMDCL), which learns deconfounded task-relevant correlations through causal intervention.
First, textual causal intervention uses back-door adjustment to disentangle spurious correlations between textual and visual modalities, thereby removing cross-modal biases in text embeddings.
Second, guided by debiased text embeddings, visual causal intervention uses front-door adjustment to block back-door paths introduced by visual confounders, improving semantic consistency and causal relevance.
By integrating these dual-causal interventions, CMDCL learns robust action representations tailored for LTAR.
The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel causality-aware LTAR framework, <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ross-<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odal <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ual-<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning (CMDCL), which leverages explicit causal interventions for vision-language fusion to uncover intricate causal structures and enable robust long-term action recognition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a textual causal intervention to reduce cross-modal bias by explicitly modeling the semantic correlations between action descriptions and corresponding atomic actions, eliminating spurious cross-modal correlations in pretrained VLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a visual causal intervention that removes visual confounders through front-door adjustment guided by debiased cross-modal knowledge, while introducing an independent visual encoder to block biased information from pretrained VLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments on the datasets, Breakfast, COIN and Charades demonstrate that CMDCL uncovers vision-language causal structures, achieving state-of-the-art performance in long-term action recognition.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>VLM-based Long-term Action Recognition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The growing demand for real-world applications has propelled long-term action recognition (LTAR) research. Early works focused on modeling temporal dependencies through handcrafted architectures predefined patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carreira and Zisserman, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hussein et al., 2019a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The attention revolution brought fundamental shifts, with spatial-temporal transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and state-space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Islam et al., 2023</a>)</cite> enabling dynamic temporal modeling. Concurrently, vision-language models (VLMs) like CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and BLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite> demonstrated text-guided visual grounding, inspiring LTAR adaptations including ActionCLIP’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> contrastive alignment and MSQNet’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mondal et al., 2023</a>)</cite> spatiotemporal queries. Recent innovations like BIKE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Text4Vis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> further bridged modalities through bidirectional knowledge transfer, whereas Video-LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> extended BLIP-2 with video-specific transformers. Efficiency-focused designs like MA-LMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> employed memory banks to handle computational complexity, marking the current state-of-the-art.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advancements, critical limitations persist in spatiotemporal causal modeling. While existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieve strong cross-modal alignment through late fusion strategies, they fundamentally neglect two fundamental aspects of atomic action relationships:
(1) Cross-modal bias propagation that disrupts temporal modeling through spurious visual-textual correlations between contextual environments and atomic actions; (2) Visual confounder propagation, allowing functionally distinct but visually similar actions to be misclassified due to shared environmental features in pretrained VLMs.
These limitations originate from modeling videos as unordered frame collections rather than temporally structured processes, allowing pretrained VLM biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to reinforce surface-level correlations (e.g., prioritizing incidental object co-occurrences over causal state transitions).
Our framework addresses these gaps by employing dual causal interventions to correct cross-modal biases and visual confounders, revealing the causal relationships critical for reliable LTAR.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Causal Representation Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Causal mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Spirtes et al., 2001</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neuberg, 2003</a>)</cite> underscore the limitations of relying solely on statistical dependencies—such as interpreting "add salt and pepper" as indicative of "making scrambled eggs"—when predicting counterfactual scenarios like "not adding salt and pepper still results in scrambled eggs." These mechanisms establish reasoning chains that facilitate predictions beyond observed distributions, thereby offering robust knowledge applicable to unseen contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In recent years, the integration of causality into video understanding has garnered increasing attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>. Causality-based methods aim to uncover invariant causal mechanisms or recover causal features to enhance video understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the application of these methods to VLM-based LTAR remains underexplored. For instance, methods like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> address video-specific biases through causal inference but lack cross-modal causal modeling, which limits their effectiveness in VLM-based action recognition. Similarly, CMCIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> employs causal reasoning for textual inference but focuses primarily on scene-object-text alignment, thereby limiting its applicability to LTAR. In this paper, we propose a cross-modal SCM tailored for VLM-based LTAR tasks. This model introduces novel dual-causal interventions designed to correct cross-modal biases and visual confounders, advancing the understanding of long-term action recognition within VLMs. We believe that the approach proposed in this paper will provide
some new ideas for subsequent work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06603v1/fig3_causal_graph_evolution.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/159;" width="476" height="159" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The proposed causal graph of causal intervention in textual and visual modalities. The solid lines represent direct influence relation, while the dash lines represent indirect influence relation. The bottom part of the figure provides an intuitive explanation of a real LTAR sample using the proposed causal intervention.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate LTAR through a causal perspective using the SCM in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Since cross-modal biases and visual confounders are inherently unobservable, we propose the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ross-<span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odal <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>ual-<span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning (CMDCL) framework (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.1. Textual Causal Intervention ‣ 3.2. Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning ‣ 3. Method ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), to model and mitigate these factors, enabling robust representation learning for LTAR.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Causal View of VLM-based LTAR</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For VLM-based LTAR, we employ an SCM to capture the causal effect between video-text pairs and their labels, as shown in Figure  <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a).
Nodes represent variables, and edges denote causal relationships.
Conventional VLM-based LTAR methods model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to Y\leftarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to Y\leftarrow T</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> represents the video, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the action text, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> the label. This approach relies on the statistical association <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|V,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|V,T)</annotation></semantics></math>, often ignoring spurious correlations induced by confounders. Our framework addresses this through dual causal pathways.
Figure  <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (bottom) presents a high-level overview of cross-modal causal intervention. Below, we provide detailed interpretations of the subgraphs:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to V\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to V\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We learn <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|V,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|V,T)</annotation></semantics></math> to focus exclusively on action-relevant features in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> correlated with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Though pretrained VLMs align <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in shared space, cross-modal biases and visual confounders create backdoor interference. Our intervention aims to remove this interference to optimize LTAR performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\leftarrow B\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\leftarrow B\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This back-door path in the textual modality arises from cross-modal bias <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by pretrained VLMs’ statistical correlations. These biases introduce spurious associations between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, embedding noncausal atomic actions (e.g., “add salt &amp; pepper”) in text features instead of true causal ones (e.g., “stirfry egg”).
By applying a do-operation on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we sever its direct causal dependency on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\leftarrow\{B,Z\}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\leftarrow\{B,Z\}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond cross-modal bias <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, visual confounders <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> create additional backdoor paths. By making <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> independent of the pretrained VLMs and blocking <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\not\to\{V,T\}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">↛</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\not\to\{V,T\}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we ensure that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|V,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|V,T)</annotation></semantics></math> is only confounded by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, introducing a mediator for performing a do-operation on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> blocks <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\not\to V\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↛</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\not\to V\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing the model to learn the true causal effect <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to Y\leftarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to Y\leftarrow T</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Figures <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c) and (d).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The model must predict <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> while avoiding spurious correlations from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, conventional models often associate “add salt &amp; pepper” with “make scrambled egg” due to frequent co-occurrence, leading to misclassification of “make fried egg” as “make scrambled egg” with high confidence.
In our SCM, the non-interventional prediction is expressed using Bayes’ rule:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:13.7pt;vertical-align:-7.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-43.3pt,1.9pt) scale(0.783754537086,0.783754537086) ;">
<p id="S3.E1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E1.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|V,T)=\sum_{Z}\sum_{B}P(Y|V,T,z,b)P(z|V,T)P(b|T)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>Z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|V,T)=\sum_{Z}\sum_{B}P(Y|V,T,z,b)P(z|V,T)P(b|T).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation captures both true causality <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to Y\leftarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to Y\leftarrow T</annotation></semantics></math> and spurious correlations arising from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\leftarrow Z\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\leftarrow Z\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V,T\}\leftarrow B\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V,T\}\leftarrow B\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying an intervention <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="do(V,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">do(V,T)</annotation></semantics></math> can eliminate these back-door paths, removing spurious correlations and deconfounding <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> to enable true causal learning.
As cross-modal bias <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and visual confounder <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is unobservable in VLM-based LTAR, this prevents direct computation of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|do(V,T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|do(V,T))</annotation></semantics></math> required for back-door adjustment. We therefore develop dual causal intervention modules to uncover causal structures and disentangle biases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed CMDCL framework (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.1. Textual Causal Intervention ‣ 3.2. Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning ‣ 3. Method ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) leverages textual and visual causal interventions to debias text embeddings, deconfound visual features, and integrate these refined features for action prediction, with detailed components described below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>Textual Causal Intervention</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address cross-modal biases in text features from pretrained VLMs, we propose the Textual Causal Intervention (TCI) module. TCI removes spurious correlations through textual back-door adjustment, targeting imbalanced co-occurrences between visual and textual features. It severs the link <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to T</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure  <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Causal Representation Learning ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b)) and identifies biased correlations, ensuring fair interaction with all potential cases of bias cases. The interventional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y\mid V,do(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y\mid V,do(T))</annotation></semantics></math> is fomulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:69.2pt;vertical-align:-30.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(51.2pt,-11.3pt) scale(1.48371634523858,1.48371634523858) ;">
<p id="S3.E2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid V,do(T))&amp;=\sum_{b\in B}P(Y\mid V,do(T),b)P(b\mid V,do(T))\\
&amp;\approx\sum_{b\in B}P(Y\mid V,do(T),b)P(b).\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid V,do(T))&amp;=\sum_{b\in B}P(Y\mid V,do(T),b)P(b\mid V,do(T))\\
&amp;\approx\sum_{b\in B}P(Y\mid V,do(T),b)P(b).\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While adjustment introduces biases to identify true label causes, correlations like “add salt &amp; pepper” co-occurring with “make scrambled egg” can obscure matching information.
To address this, our TCI module uses visual features given by pretrained VLM as prompts (i.e. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{P}=\{v_{l}^{P}\}_{l=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{P}=\{v_{l}^{P}\}_{l=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>) to query biased features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, we estimate cross-modal bias-aware scores <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{s_{c,l}\mid\text{softmax}_{l}\left((v_{l}^{P})^{\top}t_{c}/\tau\right)\}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>softmax</mtext><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{s_{c,l}\mid\text{softmax}_{l}\left((v_{l}^{P})^{\top}t_{c}/\tau\right)\}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> for learnable text embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\{t_{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{t_{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are initialized by pretrained VLMs. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of frames, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> the action classes, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding dimension, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the temperature parameter of the softmax.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">TCI fuses scores <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain bias-aware embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{b_{c}\mid\sum_{l\in L}s_{c,l}v_{l}^{P}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{b_{c}\mid\sum_{l\in L}s_{c,l}v_{l}^{P}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This fused embedding serves as a surrogate for latent bias <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, as it captures the weighted influence of biased visual features on text feature of each action category, effectively modeling the spurious correlations introduced by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The TCI module further employs a learnable approximator to fit the marginal distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B)</annotation></semantics></math> and applies the Normalized Weighted Geometric Mean (NWGM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2015</a>)</cite> to approximate debiased predictions from Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2.1. Textual Causal Intervention ‣ 3.2. Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning ‣ 3. Method ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:61.2pt;vertical-align:-27.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(41.0pt,-8.0pt) scale(1.3533364712593,1.3533364712593) ;">
<p id="S3.E3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E3.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid V,do(T))&amp;\overset{\text{NWGM}}{\approx}P(Y\mid V,\sum_{B}\text{concat}(do(T),b)P(b))\\
&amp;\approx P(Y\mid V,\sum_{c=1}^{C}h([t_{c},b_{c}]))=P(Y\mid V,T^{\prime}),\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≈</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">NWGM</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>B</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mtext>concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid V,do(T))&amp;\overset{\text{NWGM}}{\approx}P(Y\mid V,\sum_{B}\text{concat}(do(T),b)P(b))\\
&amp;\approx P(Y\mid V,\sum_{c=1}^{C}h([t_{c},b_{c}]))=P(Y\mid V,T^{\prime}),\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=\{t_{c}^{\prime}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=\{t_{c}^{\prime}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> represents debiased text embeddings after back-door adjustment, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot,\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot,\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is an approximator. Iterating all samples enables integration of textual cues and cross-modal bias factors (e.g., weakening “add salt &amp; pepper” correlations with “make scrambled egg”).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06603v1/fig4_overview_framework_w_part.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/138;" width="357" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The framework of CMDCL. CMDCL leverages pretrained VLMs for cross-modal knowledge extraction and employs causal interventions to mitigate cross-modal biases and visual confounders, improving LTAR performance. The Textual Causal Intervention module debiases text embeddings using back-door adjustment by calculating bias-aware scores and approximating marginal distributions of the biases. The Visual Causal Intervention module deconfounds visual embeddings via front-door adjustment, utilizing spatio-temporal attention, fine-grained information scoring, and an approximator, facilitated by a video encoder independent of VLMs that blocks visual back-door paths from VLMs. The Classifier combines debiased textual and deconfounded visual features to classify long-term actions.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Visual Causal Intervention</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the visual modality under the influence of cross-modal bias <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and visual confounders <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. In the Visual Causal Intervention (VCI) module, a video encoder independent of VLMs is introduced to extract video features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively blocking <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to V</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> remains intrinsic. Notably, cross-modal bias and visual confounders operate at different scales: cross-modal bias stems from coarse-grained spurious associations between textual descriptions and atomic actions, whereas visual confounders involve fine-grained elements such as lighting and attire. These fine-grained confounders are challenging to localize in visual features, making the back-door adjustment in TCI, which relies on predefined confounders, ineffective for mitigating back-door paths caused by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this challenge, the VCI module employs a front-door adjustment strategy guided by debiased text embeddings.
Specifically, VCI assumes that TCI provides debiased text embeddings, denoted as the mediator <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The suitability of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a mediator stems from the following properties: 1) Debiased text embeddings represent intrinsic features of long-term actions, and extracting these features from visual modality is feasible, establishing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. 2) As <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is derived from textual modality and independent of visual confounders <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no back-door paths between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> involving <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. 3) In VLM-based LTAR models, the classification task is effectively reframed as a matching task between visual and textual features, ensuring <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, VCI introduces the mediator <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming the path <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to M\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to M\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> to enable front-door adjustment. The interventional probability is equal to the conditional one:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid do(V))=P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid do(V))=P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By intervening on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, VCI can block the back-door path <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\leftarrow Z\to V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\leftarrow Z\to V\to M</annotation></semantics></math> , even when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is unobservable:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E5.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:19.1pt;vertical-align:-10.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(12.3pt,-0.7pt) scale(1.08524127751547,1.08524127751547) ;">
<p id="S3.E5.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E5.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y\mid do(M=t_{c}^{\prime}),T)=\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}P(V=\hat{v}_{c})P(Y\mid V=\hat{v}_{c},M=t_{c}^{\prime},T)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y\mid do(M=t_{c}^{\prime}),T)=\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}P(V=\hat{v}_{c})P(Y\mid V=\hat{v}_{c},M=t_{c}^{\prime},T),</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{V}=\{\hat{v}_{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{V}=\{\hat{v}_{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of visually emphasized features when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{c}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{c}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the mediator, isolating the direct effect of visual features on the outcome, hence ensuring the causal estimate is not confounded by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
To derive <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, VCI employs spatio-temporal attention mechanisms to enhance video representation and compute fine-grained, information-aware scores. Given <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, the process involves applying multi-head self-attention across <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> layers followed by layer normalization. The output <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{(H)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{(H)}</annotation></semantics></math> is then utilized to calculate the scores <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, with each score <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c,l,d}^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c,l,d}^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> computed using the formula <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c,l,d}^{F}=\text{softmax}(v_{l,d}^{(H)}t_{c,d}^{\prime}\,/\,\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c,l,d}^{F}=\text{softmax}(v_{l,d}^{(H)}t_{c,d}^{\prime}\,/\,\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the temperature parameter. Each <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is finally defined as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{l=1}^{L}s_{c,l,d}^{F}v_{l,d}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l=1}^{L}s_{c,l,d}^{F}v_{l,d}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively isolating key features for causal analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By introducing debiased text embeddings as the mediator, VCI applies the front-door adjustment to perform the do-operation on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, eliminating the interference of visual confounders on the causal estimation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, through combining Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2.2. Visual Causal Intervention ‣ 3.2. Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning ‣ 3. Method ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2.2. Visual Causal Intervention ‣ 3.2. Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning ‣ 3. Method ‣ Cross-Modal Dual-Causal Learning for Long-Term Action Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we have:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E6.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:68.6pt;vertical-align:-31.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(38.2pt,-8.4pt) scale(1.32181148171673,1.32181148171673) ;">
<p id="S3.E6.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E6.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} &amp;P(Y\mid do(V),T)=\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}P(M=t^{\prime}\mid do(V))P(Y\mid do(M=t^{\prime}),T)\\
&amp;=\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)P(V=\hat{v}_{c})P(Y\mid M=t_{c}^{\prime},V=\hat{v}_{c},T).\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} &amp;P(Y\mid do(V),T)=\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}P(M=t^{\prime}\mid do(V))P(Y\mid do(M=t^{\prime}),T)\\
&amp;=\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)P(V=\hat{v}_{c})P(Y\mid M=t_{c}^{\prime},V=\hat{v}_{c},T).\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify modeling, we adopt NWGM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, incorporating sampling at the feature level:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E7.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:46.8pt;vertical-align:-20.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(30.5pt,-4.5pt) scale(1.24152261812821,1.24152261812821) ;">
<p id="S3.E7.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E7.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid do(V),T)\overset{\text{NWGM}}{\approx}P(Y\mid\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}\text{concat}(M=t_{c}^{\prime},V=\hat{v}_{c},)\\
\phantom{P(}\times P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)P(V=\hat{v}_{c}),T).\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>≈</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">NWGM</mtext></mover><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mtext>concat</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} P(Y\mid do(V),T)\overset{\text{NWGM}}{\approx}P(Y\mid\sum_{t_{c}^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}}\sum_{\hat{v}_{c}\in\hat{V}}\text{concat}(M=t_{c}^{\prime},V=\hat{v}_{c},)\\
\phantom{P(}\times P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)P(V=\hat{v}_{c}),T).\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, VCI employs a learnable approximator <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(M=t_{c}^{\prime}\mid V)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(V=\hat{v}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(V=\hat{v}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces deconfounded visual embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}=\{v_{c}^{\prime}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}=\{v_{c}^{\prime}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E8.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:18.4pt;vertical-align:-10.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.6pt,-0.0pt) scale(1.00362116286463,1.00362116286463) ;">
<p id="S3.E8.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E8.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y\mid do(V),T)\approx P(Y\mid\sum_{c=1}^{C}g([t_{c}^{\prime},\hat{v}_{c}]),V)=P(Y\mid V^{\prime},T)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y\mid do(V),T)\approx P(Y\mid\sum_{c=1}^{C}g([t_{c}^{\prime},\hat{v}_{c}]),V)=P(Y\mid V^{\prime},T).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3. </span>Classifier</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining deconfounded visual embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and debiased text embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we feed them into the classifier module for long-term action recognition. The predicted probability for class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:20.2pt;vertical-align:-9.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-14.0pt,0.9pt) scale(0.91822753355808,0.91822753355808) ;">
<p id="S3.E9.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E9.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y=c\mid\text{do}(V,T))=\text{Softmax}\Bigl(\sum_{d=1}^{D}w_{c,d}\cdot f(T^{\prime},V^{\prime})_{c,d}+b_{c}\Bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y=c\mid\text{do}(V,T))=\text{Softmax}\Bigl(\sum_{d=1}^{D}w_{c,d}\cdot f(T^{\prime},V^{\prime})_{c,d}+b_{c}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{c,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{c,d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> are the learnable parameters for class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cross-modal interaction function.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>
Comparisons of Acc (%) and mAP (%) with SOTA methods on Breakfast and COIN. Breakfast evaluates top-1 Acc (Acc@1) while COIN reports both Acc@1 and top-5 Acc (Acc@5). FLOPs (G, <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{9}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Params (M, <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>) denote inference-phase computational costs.
</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:124.3pt;vertical-align:-61.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-198.2pt,71.4pt) scale(0.465309567269832,0.465309567269832) ;">
